# AGENTS.md — {{name}}

This is an **operonx** project (made with operonx {{version}}). The logic is
Python functions (ops), wired into graphs, which an application runs as
jobs{{#http}} and services{{/http}}. `app/main.py` is the map of the product:
read it first.

## How to work here

1. Start from the code that is already here and from the **recipes** below.
   They are the tested idioms for the usual changes.
2. Need something the recipes do not show, or an error you do not
   understand? Search the guide, then read only that section:
   `grep -n "WORD" .operonx/guide/*/*.md`. The index is
   `.operonx/guide/README.md`; the pages named below (`Guide: 01-ops.md`)
   are in `.operonx/guide/core/`. Every guide example is tested against the
   installed operonx, so copy from it; never write an operonx API from memory.
3. Verify with `{{run}}operonx check`: lint, the tests, every graph built
   offline{{#http}}, every route reached (a `GET` called; `--call` also calls
   each `POST` once with a fake model){{/http}}, in one verdict. A `FAIL` line
   says what to fix. Then run what it cannot judge (a job's output, a page's
   look). Write a test when the task asks for one or when you fix a bug.

## Layout

- `src/<feature>/`: `graph.py` (wiring only: no logic, no I/O, no Python
  `if`), `ops.py` (the logic), `jobs.py`{{#http}}, `routes.py`{{/http}}{{#chat}}, `prompts.py`{{/chat}}{{#agent}}, `agent.py`{{/agent}}; anything
  else `_<name>.py`. Shared code is `src/_shared/`; `src/_shared/config.py`
  is the only file that reads the environment (`ROOT`, `DATA`, `OUT`{{#http}}, `PORT`{{/http}});
  a new setting is one more line there: `LIMIT = env("LIMIT", 10)`.
- `app/main.py`: `APP = Application(...)` lists every job{{#http}} and service{{/http}}.
- Models and stores by key in `resources.yaml`; secrets in `.env` as
  `${VAR}`, every name also in `.env.example`. Never build a client in code.
- `data/` is kept, `out/` is throwaway. Tests: `tests/<feature>/`.{{#web}}
- Pages: `web/` at `/`, one `pages/<screen>.js` per screen, `components/`
  render only, `lib/api.js` is the only `fetch`.{{/web}}
- Guide: `05-project-layout.md` § The files.

## Recipes

**Start a feature with `{{run}}operonx add`**: it writes the op, the graph and
their wiring{{#http}} into `APP`{{/http}}, then prints the `TODO` lines to fill in. What it writes
passes `operonx check` as it is.

```bash
{{#http}}{{run}}operonx add route create_ticket POST /tickets --in text --llm  # model call, then an op
{{run}}operonx add route find_ticket GET /tickets/{id}   # {id} is an input
{{/http}}{{run}}operonx add job daily_summary --llm --at 07:00      # a Job (on a clock)
{{run}}operonx add llm classify --feature tickets           # a model call, as a graph
{{#web}}{{run}}operonx add page stats --data /tickets              # web/stats/: a table, linked from /
{{/web}}```

`--feature F` picks the `src/` package{{#http}} (a route: the path's first part){{/http}}. A model call
turns on `llm:assistant`, its `.env.example` names, the `openai` extra and a fake
model for the tests.

An op is one step; it returns a dict literal. A graph wires ops with `>>`;
its parameters are its inputs (a job item{{#http}}, a request body{{/http}}), and the outputs
of the ops wired to `END` are its answer. See `src/` for a working pair.
Guide: `01-ops.md` § `@op`, § `@graph`; `04-gotchas.md` (read it once).

**A model call**: uncomment `llm:assistant` in `resources.yaml`; a project
with no model yet needs `{{add}} "operonx[openai]"` (the fake model too).

```python
from operonx.providers.ops import LLMOp


@graph
def summarise(text):
    ask = LLMOp.of(
        resource="assistant", prompt={"system": "One line.", "user": "{text}"}, text=text
    )
    START >> ask >> END  # ask["content"]; ask["error"] is set on failure
```

`fields=["intent: str"]` parses the answer into its own output;
`stream=True` streams. Never `user=` and never a Ref in `validators=`.
Guide: `01-ops.md` § `LLMOp`; `04-gotchas.md` § `LLMOp`.

**A branch**: `START >> c >> if_(c["big"] == True, a).else_(b)`. The arms merge
by themselves. Compare a Ref with a literal; combine with `&` `|` `~`, never
`and`/`or`. Guide: `03-control-flow.md` § if/else; `04-gotchas.md` § Combine
conditions.

**Retry a flaky model call**: on its resource, never a loop in an op:

```yaml
llm:assistant:
  retry: {max_attempts: 3, initial: 0.5, max_interval: 10}
```

A flaky op: `@op(retry=Retry(max_attempts=3))`. A whole project: `[retry]` in
`operonx.toml`. Guide: `06-failures.md` (also timeouts and error edges).

**A job**: `Job("name", graph=g, items=DATA / "x.jsonl", key="id",
output=OUT / "x.jsonl")`. `items` can also be a list or a function that yields
dicts. `operonx run name --resume` skips done ids. Add
`schedule=schedule(at="07:00")` (or `every="5m"`) to run it on a clock
inside `operonx serve`. Guide: `02-composition.md` § Job, § A job on a clock.
{{#http}}

**A route**: `Service("name", http("POST", "/path", port=PORT), graph=g)` in
`routes.py`. The JSON body fills the graph's parameters by name, and so does
the path: `http("GET", "/tickets/{id}")` passes `id`. An unknown field is
answered `400 {"field": ...}`. The reply is the graph's answer with `200`,
unless an op outputs `"$status"` (the key is not sent):

```python
@op
def find(id: str) -> dict:
    ticket = TICKETS.get(id)
    return {"ticket": ticket, "$status": 200 if ticket else 404}  # 201 for a create
```

Every route is a `Service` (lint C14), so every request has a trace: no
hand-written Starlette route, and never unpack a request dict in an op.
{{#web}}List the `web` service (`asgi("/")`) last in `APP`: a mount at `/`
answers every path, so a route after it is never reached.
{{/web}}Guide: `02-composition.md` § Service; streams and websockets: `01-ops.md`
§ Doors.
{{/http}}

**Test**: call an op directly, `my_op(x=1)()`. Run a graph with
`asyncio.run(Job("t", graph=g, items=[...], key="id", record_dir=tmp_path).run())`
and check `run.status == "ok"` and `run.results`.{{#http}} For a route:
`TestClient(Application.find(ROOT).asgi())`.{{/http}} Never call a real
model: a `conftest.py` fixture installs a fake one.

```python
FAKE = "llm:assistant:\n  api_type: fake\n  script:\n    - echo: 'Echo: '\n"


@pytest.fixture(scope="session")
def fake_model(tmp_path_factory):
    path = tmp_path_factory.mktemp("res") / "resources.yaml"
    path.write_text(FAKE)
    operonx.bootstrap(resources=path, env=False)
```

`script:` turns can also be a string, `{status: 503}` or `{delay: 0.5, text: ...}`.
Guide: `01-ops.md` § `LLMOp` (Tests need no model).

**Why a run failed or is slow**: an op that raises does not raise. The
result carries `"$errors"`, and the run's record is under `.operonx/runs/`.
Guide: `08-runs.md` (traces, slow runs, durable runs); `04-gotchas.md`
§ An op that raises.
{{brd}}
## Commands

<!-- operonx:commands -->
{{shell}}```bash
{{run}}operonx add WHAT NAME   # route, job, llm, page: wired code + what to fill in
{{run}}operonx check          # lint, tests, graphs, routes: one verdict (--call, --no-tests)
{{run}}pytest                 # the tests: keep them green
{{run}}operonx lint           # the rules below, checked (operonx lint --rules)
{{run}}operonx serve --list   # every service, and where it listens
{{run}}operonx run --list     # every job
{{run}}operonx serve          # serve every service (and run scheduled jobs on their clocks)
{{run}}operonx run NAME       # run one job
```
<!-- /operonx:commands -->

## Rules

- Every `@graph` at module level, never inside a function; a setting is a
  graph input, a different shape is another graph.
- Never `print()`: `from operonx.core.loggings import LOGGER`.
- Names come from variables (`reply = chat(text=text)` is `reply`); `name=`
  only when other code reads it; one op per line.
- No retry loop and no "save progress" code in an op: `retry:`/`[retry]`
  and `[durable]` do it.
- Imports go one way: `app/` → `graph.py` → `ops.py` → helpers; `src/`
  never imports `app/`.

